WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.321
You know the size to look for.

2
00:00:01.321 --> 00:00:07.000
Now let's actually get a model — the app's own download path, separate from Ollama or LM Studio, straight from Library, Discover.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.990
Library, Discover.

4
00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:14.865
Search a GGUF model by name, or skip search entirely and paste a Hugging Face URL directly — the resolve link ending in the .

5
00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:15.635
gguf filename.

6
00:00:15.635 --> 00:00:18.000
Either way lands on the same download step.

7
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.644
Start the download and watch the progress bar.

8
00:00:20.644 --> 00:00:28.000
It lands under the harnessx models folder in your home directory, ready to load — no separate model manager, no Ollama required.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:39.000
My Models lists everything downloaded, lets you set which one loads by default, and shows a “will it fit” indicator tied to the RAM and VRAM the app actually detected on this machine — a straight answer before you commit to a load.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.605
A little of the Rust underneath, for credibility.

11
00:00:40.605 --> 00:00:44.207
hw_info detects total RAM, AVX2 support, and an NVIDIA GPU via nvidia-smi — that is what powers the fit check.

12
00:00:44.207 --> 00:00:45.287
Downloads stream to a temporary .

13
00:00:45.287 --> 00:00:47.088
part file and only rename to the final name on success.

14
00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:47.972
And archives — zip and tar.

15
00:00:47.972 --> 00:00:52.000
gz — are extracted in-process, with path-traversal protection so a malicious archive can't write outside the models folder.

16
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.365
Gotcha: a green “will it fit” only covers loading the model into memory — it says nothing about tool-calling reliability.

17
00:00:57.365 --> 00:01:01.000
Come back to E48's README test before trusting a small model with real agent work.

18
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.640
Downloaded is not the same as running.

19
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.000
Next we load this model into the app's own supervised server and tune context length and GPU offload.

